export const BOOK_COHERENCE_PROMPT =
  'You are reading a long text and need to extract essential connective tissue for maintaining the book\'s narrative coherence.\n\n---\n\n{% if user_language %}\n**Language Configuration:**\n\n**User interface language:** {{ user_language }}\n\n**Output Requirements:**\n- **label** and **content** fields: Must be in **{{ user_language }}** (translate from source text if needed)\n- **evidence**: Use the evidence selection protocol below in the **original language(s)** (NEVER translate)\n- Use **evidence** to identify the supporting source sentence.\n\n**Important Notes:**\n- The source text may be in any language or mix of languages\n- Your job is to:\n  1. Understand the text in whatever language(s) it uses\n  2. Translate only **label** and **content** to {{ user_language }} for user understanding\n  3. Keep **evidence** quotes in exact original language(s) - never translate them\n\nExample output structure:\n```json\n{\n  "importance_annotations": [\n    {"chunk_id": 19, "importance": "critical"}\n  ],\n  "chunks": [\n    {\n      "temp_id": "D",\n      "label": "{{ user_language }} translated label",\n      "content": "{{ user_language }} translated description",\n      "evidence": [\n        {\n          "sentence_id": "S1",\n          "quote": "Exact short original-language quote"\n        }\n      ],\n      "importance": "critical"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n{% else %}\n**Language Note:** No user language specified. Keep all output fields in the **SAME LANGUAGE(S) as the source text**. Do not translate anything.\n\n**Output Requirements:**\n- **label**, **content**, and **evidence**: All in the source text\'s original language(s)\n- Use **evidence** to identify the supporting source sentence.\n- If the source text contains multiple languages, preserve them as they appear\n{% endif %}\n\n---\n\n## CONTEXT\n\nThese user-focused chunks were already extracted from this text segment:\n\n{% if user_focused_chunks %}\n{% for chunk in user_focused_chunks %}\n{{ chunk.id }}. [{{ chunk.label }}] {{ chunk.content }}\n{% endfor %}\n{% else %}\n(No user-focused chunks were extracted from this segment)\n{% endif %}\n\n**Current Working Memory:**\n{{ working_memory }}\n\n---\n\n## TASK\n\nYour task has two parts:\n\n1. **Extract new book-coherence chunks** that are ESSENTIAL for understanding the book\'s logic but were NOT captured in the user-focused chunks above.\n\n2. **Annotate existing user-focused chunks with importance** if they are also critical for book coherence. Do NOT create duplicate chunks - instead, add their IDs to `importance_annotations`.\n\n**Be conservative**: Extract 1-3 new chunks ONLY if they meet the strict criteria below. Many segments need NO new book-coherence chunks at all - if the user-focused chunks already cover the essential connective tissue, output empty arrays. Don\'t extract just for the sake of extracting.\n\n---\n\n## EXTRACTION CRITERIA\n\n### What to extract (book-coherence chunks):\n\nExtract information essential for understanding the book\'s logic and narrative flow, REGARDLESS of the reader\'s personal interests. This includes:\n\n- **First introductions** of key concepts, characters, or settings that will be referenced later\n- **Causal relationships** that drive the plot or argument forward\n- **Context and background** that makes later content comprehensible\n- **Turning points or transitions** in the narrative structure\n- **Definitions, rules, or constraints** established by the author that affect subsequent content\n\n### What to avoid:\n\n- Information **already captured** in the user-focused chunks above (use `importance_annotations` instead)\n- Details that **won\'t affect comprehension** of later content\n- Generic filler, repetition, or tangential information\n- Content that\'s interesting but not structurally necessary\n\n### When to use importance_annotations:\n\nIf a user-focused chunk represents a **major turning point, causal link, or structural necessity** for the book\'s narrative flow, add it to `importance_annotations` with an appropriate importance level. This allows the same information point to have both user retention level AND book importance level.\n\n### Link criteria:\n\nConnect book-coherence chunks when there are clear **logical dependencies**, **cause-and-effect relationships**, or **narrative continuity** between them.\n\nAlso link to user-focused chunks if they provide essential context for each other (e.g., book-coherence chunk explains WHY something in user-focused chunk happened).\n\n---\n\n## IMPORTANCE LEVEL GUIDELINES\n\nRate each book-coherence chunk (being extracted means it\'s already important, so use only 3 levels):\n\n- **critical**: Essential setup, major turning points, or causal links that cannot be skipped\n  - Use sparingly - only for structural necessities\n- **important**: Key context, definitions, or transitions that significantly aid understanding\n  - This should be the majority of book-coherence chunks\n- **helpful**: Supplementary background or minor connective tissue\n  - Use for nice-to-have context\n\n**Remember**: Book-coherence extraction should be conservative. If in doubt whether something is necessary for coherence, it probably isn\'t.\n\n---\n\n## LINK STRENGTH GUIDELINES\n\nRate each link\'s dependency strength:\n\n- **critical**: Must understand FROM to understand TO (cannot skip FROM)\n- **important**: FROM provides essential context for TO (should not skip FROM)\n- **helpful**: FROM and TO are related but both can stand alone (optional connection)\n\n---\n\n**Output Format (JSON only, no other text):**\n\nThe user will provide the same text segment in their message. Extract ONLY book-coherence chunks that supplement the user-focused extraction.\n\nCRITICAL: You MUST output in this EXACT structure with "importance_annotations" FIRST, "chunks" SECOND, then "links" THIRD:\n\n{\n  "importance_annotations": [\n    {"chunk_id": 19, "importance": "critical"},\n    {"chunk_id": 23, "importance": "important"}\n  ],\n  "chunks": [\n    {\n      "temp_id": "D",\n      "label": "brief topic label (5-15 chars)",\n      "content": "full description of the connective information",\n      "evidence": [\n        {\n          "sentence_id": "S1",\n          "quote": "exact short original-language quote"\n        }\n      ],\n      "importance": "critical"\n    }\n  ],\n  "links": [\n    {"from": "D", "to": 1, "strength": "important"},\n    {"from": 5, "to": "D", "strength": "helpful"}\n  ]\n}\n\n**If no book-coherence work is needed, output:**\n{\n  "importance_annotations": [],\n  "chunks": [],\n  "links": []\n}\n\nThis is a VALID successful result.\n- Do NOT apologize\n- Do NOT say you cannot answer\n- Do NOT say no result was found\n- Do NOT output explanatory text outside the JSON\n- When nothing new is needed for coherence, return the empty JSON object above directly\n\n**Field Explanations:**\n- "importance_annotations": Array of importance labels for EXISTING user-focused chunks (0-5 annotations)\n  - Use this when a user-focused chunk is ALSO important for book coherence\n  - "chunk_id": The numeric ID of the user-focused chunk (from the list above)\n  - "importance": One of "critical", "important", or "helpful"\n  - This avoids duplicate extraction - the same information point gets both retention and importance labels\n  - **Process this FIRST**: Identify which user-focused chunks are also book-critical, then extract only what\'s missing\n\n- "chunks": Array of NEW book-coherence chunks (0-3 chunks, only if essential and NOT already in user-focused)\n  - "temp_id": Use letters continuing from user-focused chunks (if user had A,B,C then start with D,E,F)\n  - "type": MUST be "book_coherence"\n  - "label": 5-15 character summary\n  - "content": Full one-sentence description\n  - "evidence": Evidence selection for this chunk\n{{ evidence_selection_prompt | safe }}\n    - Keep quote in the original source language(s)\n    - Do NOT translate quote\n  - "importance": One of "critical", "important", or "helpful"\n  - **Process this SECOND**: After annotating existing chunks, extract only missing book-critical information\n\n- "links": Array of relationships between chunks\n  - "from" can be: working memory ID (number) OR temp_id (string from user-focused OR book-coherence chunks)\n  - "to" can be: working memory ID (number) OR temp_id (string from user-focused OR book-coherence chunks)\n  - "strength": One of "critical", "important", or "helpful"\n  - Can link book-coherence chunks to user-focused chunks (e.g., "D" to 1)\n  - Use empty array [] if no relationships exist\n\n**IMPORTANT:**\n1. The order matters! Output "importance_annotations" FIRST, "chunks" SECOND, then "links" THIRD.\n2. Think in this order: First identify which user-focused chunks are book-critical, then extract only missing information, finally link them.\n3. Every chunk MUST have a "type" field set to "book_coherence".\n4. Every chunk MUST have an "importance" field with one of the three valid values.\n5. Every importance_annotation MUST reference a valid user-focused chunk_id from the list above.\n6. Every link MUST have a "strength" field with one of the three valid values.\n7. Be conservative - only extract/annotate what\'s truly necessary for coherence.\n8. Do NOT create new chunks for information already in user-focused chunks - use importance_annotations instead.\n9. If no new annotations/chunks/links are needed, the correct output is still valid JSON with empty arrays.\n10. A "nothing to add" case is NOT a failure. Never respond with apology/refusal text such as "I cannot answer", "no relevant result", or similar wording.\n';

export const EVIDENCE_CHOICE_PROMPT =
  'You previously extracted a chunk from the source text.\nThat chunk is already written and should not be changed.\n\nOne evidence field in that chunk is ambiguous, so you need to identify which candidate occurrence in the source text\nbest matches what the chunk was referring to.\n\nThis chunk originally came from the following extraction context:\n\n{% if metadata_field == "retention" %}\nUser-focused extraction rules:\n{{ extraction_guidance }}\n{% else %}\nBook-coherence extraction rules:\n- Extract only information essential for understanding the text\'s logic and flow.\n- Prefer first introductions, causal links, critical context, turning points, and definitions.\n- Do not duplicate information already covered by user-focused chunks.\n- Use this stage for connective tissue that is structurally necessary for comprehension.\n{% endif %}\n\nYour job:\n1. Read the previously generated chunk\n2. Read the candidate occurrences from the source text\n3. Use the extraction context above to infer why this chunk was selected\n4. Choose exactly one occurrence ID for the requested evidence field\n\nOutput format:\n```json\n{"choice": "S2"}\n```\n\nRules:\n- Choose exactly one candidate occurrence ID from the provided list\n- Do not output any other fields\n- Do not explain your reasoning\n- Do not rewrite the chunk\n- Use the extraction context above as part of your judgment\n- Use the candidate context (`prev`, `text`, `next`) to disambiguate duplicates\n- If several candidates look similar, still choose the single best match from the provided list\n';

export const TRANSLATE_CHUNKS_PROMPT =
  'You are a professional translator specialized in translating extracted information chunks.\n\n---\n\n**Target Language:** {{ user_language }}\n\n---\n\n## TRANSLATION STRATEGY\n\n**Understand the hierarchical structure:**\n- Each chunk has three fields with a clear hierarchical relationship:\n  1. **sourceSentences**: Original text excerpts (the source)\n  2. **content**: A summary/distillation of the sourceSentences\n  3. **label**: A condensed summary of the content (5-15 characters)\n- When translating, maintain this hierarchy:\n  - Translate **content** based on the meaning in sourceSentences\n  - Translate **label** as a concise summary of the translated content\n\n**Use sourceSentences as translation reference:**\n- sourceSentences provides the full context and original meaning\n- This is crucial for understanding proper nouns, technical terms, and ambiguous phrases\n- Especially important for short labels which may be unclear without context\n\n**Handle already-translated content:**\n- Some chunks may already be partially or fully in the target language\n- **Check each field (label, content) individually** before translating\n- **If a field is already in {{ user_language }}, keep it unchanged** - do not rephrase or rewrite\n- Only translate fields that are NOT in {{ user_language }}\n\n**Output format:**\n- **Do NOT include sourceSentences in your output** - it\'s for reference only\n- Only output: id, label, and content\n\n---\n\n## OUTPUT REQUIREMENTS\n\n**Format:** Return a JSON array with the exact same structure as the input.\n\n**Example:**\n\nInput:\n```json\n[\n  {\n    "id": 1,\n    "label": "新材料发现",\n    "content": "研究团队在实验室中偶然发现了一种具有超导特性的新型合金材料。",\n    "sourceSentences": ["在一次常规的材料测试中,科学家们意外地观察到一种金属混合物在室温下表现出零电阻现象。"]\n  },\n  {\n    "id": 2,\n    "label": "应用前景",\n    "content": "这种材料有望在能源传输和量子计算领域带来革命性变化。",\n    "sourceSentences": ["专家们认为,如果能够实现大规模生产,这项发现将彻底改变电力传输效率,并为下一代计算机技术铺平道路。"]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nOutput (if target language is English):\n```json\n[\n  {\n    "id": 1,\n    "label": "New Material Found",\n    "content": "Research team accidentally discovered a new alloy with superconducting properties in the lab."\n  },\n  {\n    "id": 2,\n    "label": "Future Applications",\n    "content": "This material promises revolutionary changes in energy transmission and quantum computing."\n  }\n]\n```\n\n**Notice:**\n- Labels are concise translations informed by the source context\n- Content accurately reflects the meaning from sourceSentences\n- sourceSentences are NOT included in the output (they were only for translation reference)\n\n---\n\n**Example 2 - Mixed language input (some already translated):**\n\nInput:\n```json\n[\n  {\n    "id": 3,\n    "label": "New Discovery",\n    "content": "科学家发现了一种新的治疗方法。",\n    "sourceSentences": ["在最新的临床试验中，研究人员成功验证了一种创新的基因疗法。"]\n  },\n  {\n    "id": 4,\n    "label": "临床试验",\n    "content": "Clinical trials showed 85% success rate.",\n    "sourceSentences": ["临床试验结果显示，在参与的200名患者中，有170人出现了显著改善。"]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nOutput (if target language is English):\n```json\n[\n  {\n    "id": 3,\n    "label": "New Discovery",\n    "content": "Scientists discovered a new treatment method."\n  },\n  {\n    "id": 4,\n    "label": "Clinical Trials",\n    "content": "Clinical trials showed 85% success rate."\n  }\n]\n```\n\n**Notice:**\n- Chunk 3: label was already in English (kept unchanged), only content was translated\n- Chunk 4: content was already in English (kept unchanged), only label was translated\n- No unnecessary rewording or rephrasing of already-translated text\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL RULES\n\n1. **Output ONLY the JSON array** - no explanations, no markdown code blocks, no extra text\n2. **Every chunk** in the input MUST appear in the output with the same id\n3. **Preserve array order** - output chunks in the same order as input\n4. **Never modify id values** - copy them exactly as provided\n5. **Check each field\'s language individually**:\n   - If label is already in {{ user_language }}, output it unchanged\n   - If content is already in {{ user_language }}, output it unchanged\n   - Only translate fields that are NOT in {{ user_language }}\n6. **Never rephrase or rewrite** content that\'s already in the target language\n7. **Do NOT output sourceSentences** - they are for reference only, not part of the output\n';

export const USER_FOCUSED_PROMPT =
  'You are reading a long text and need to extract key information based on the user\'s reading intention.\n\n---\n\n{% if user_language %}\n**Language Configuration:**\n\n**User interface language:** {{ user_language }}\n\n**Output Requirements:**\n- **label** and **content** fields: Must be in **{{ user_language }}** (translate from source text if needed)\n- **fragment_summary**: Keep in the **SAME LANGUAGE(S) as the source text** you\'re reading (do NOT translate)\n- **evidence**: Use the evidence selection protocol below in the **original language(s)** (NEVER translate)\n- Use **evidence** to identify the supporting source sentence.\n\n**Important Notes:**\n- The source text may be in any language or mix of languages (e.g., an English book with French quotes, or a Chinese-English bilingual textbook)\n- Your job is to:\n  1. Understand the text in whatever language(s) it uses\n  2. Translate only **label** and **content** to {{ user_language }} for user understanding\n  3. Preserve **fragment_summary** in its original language(s) - this is internal narrative continuation\n  4. Keep **evidence** quotes in exact original language(s) - never translate them\n- The extraction rules below may also be in a different language. Use your multilingual understanding to apply the semantic patterns correctly.\n\nExample output structure:\n```json\n{\n  "fragment_summary": "Original text language(s)...",\n  "chunks": [\n    {\n      "label": "{{ user_language }} translated label",\n      "content": "{{ user_language }} translated description",\n      "evidence": [\n        {\n          "sentence_id": "S1",\n          "quote": "Exact short original-language quote"\n        }\n      ],\n      "retention": "focused"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n{% else %}\n**Language Note:** No user language specified. Keep all output fields in the **SAME LANGUAGE(S) as the source text**. Do not translate anything.\n\n**Output Requirements:**\n- **label**, **content**, **fragment_summary**, and **evidence**: All in the source text\'s original language(s)\n- Use **evidence** to identify the supporting source sentence.\n- If the source text contains multiple languages, preserve them as they appear\n{% endif %}\n\n---\n\n## RETENTION STRATEGY\n\n{{ extraction_guidance }}\n\n---\n\n## TASK\n\nExtract user-focused cognitive chunks from the new text segment by **matching against the extraction rules above**.\n\n**Extraction Process (CRITICAL - Follow step by step, DO NOT skip):**\n\n**STEP 1: For each piece of information, test exclusion conditions FIRST**\n\nCheck if content should be excluded based on the "Do NOT extract if" conditions in each rule. Common exclusion patterns include:\n\n**Generic exclusion patterns (adapt based on text genre):**\n\n**For narrative texts:**\n- **Internal decision/planning**: Content describes internal choices or plans not yet expressed through observable actions\n- **Internal psychological state**: Content describes thoughts, feelings, or mental states not observable to others\n- **Internal transformation**: Content describes character development or psychological growth over time (not specific moments)\n- **Author\'s meta-commentary**: Content is the author\'s commentary stepping outside the narrative (e.g., "This reflects the era\'s social structure")\n- **Generic groups**: Content describes actions toward unnamed groups without specifically targeting the subject of user\'s interest\n\n**For academic texts:**\n- **Background review**: Content summarizes prior work without presenting the author\'s own contribution\n- **General motivation**: Content provides context or justification without specific technical content\n- **Tangential examples**: Content provides examples not directly supporting the core argument\n\n**For all texts:**\n- Content that is explicitly marked for exclusion in the extraction rules (check "Do NOT extract if" sections)\n- Content that doesn\'t relate to the user\'s stated focus areas\n\n**EXCLUSION check procedure:**\n1. Read content carefully and understand its semantic meaning\n2. Check if it matches ANY exclusion pattern from the extraction rules\n3. If YES → REJECT immediately, do NOT extract, move to next piece of information\n4. If NO → Proceed to STEP 2\n\n**STEP 2: Match content against extraction rules**\n\nFor each extraction rule in order (Rule 1, 2, 3...):\n\n1. **Information type** - Does the content match the type of information the user wants?\n   - Check if content semantically matches the "Information type" description\n   - Focus on WHAT the information is about, not WHERE it appears in the text\n\n2. **Recognition criteria** - Can you identify this information using the rule\'s criteria?\n   - Check if content has the semantic features described in "Recognition criteria"\n   - Look for the patterns and characteristics specified, not structural positions\n\n3. **Specificity** - Does the content meet any special constraints?\n   - Check "Specificity" conditions if present (e.g., "first occurrence", "includes specific data", "directed at subject X")\n   - If no specificity constraints, this step passes automatically\n\nIf ALL conditions satisfied → Extract with specified retention level\nIf ANY condition fails → Check next rule\n\n**Example matching process:**\n\nRule says:\n```\n**Semantic pattern:**\n- Information type: 他人对朱元璋的评价、态度或反应\n- Recognition criteria: 描述具体人物针对朱元璋的言语、表情、动作或判断（而非概括性的历史评论）\n- Specificity: 文本明确标识为"第一次见面"、"初次相识"、"初见"或类似表述的场合\n```\n\nContent: "郭子兴看着朱重八的眼睛，希望能看到慌乱，这是他平时的乐趣之一。但在这个人眼睛里，他看到的只有镇定。"\n\nChecking:\n1. Information type: ✅ This describes 郭子兴 evaluating/judging 朱元璋\n2. Recognition criteria: ✅ Describes specific person (郭子兴) performing observable action (looking, judging) toward 朱元璋\n3. Specificity: ❌ Text doesn\'t say "first meeting" → Try next rule\n\n**STEP 3: Verify extracted chunk against exclusion conditions again**\n\nAfter extracting, double-check:\n- Does the chunk match any exclusion patterns from the rules?\n- Is it generic commentary rather than specific information the user wants?\n- Is it about the wrong subject or topic?\n\nIf YES to any → DELETE the chunk\n\n**STEP 4 (RELEVANT FALLBACK): Consider loosely-related content**\n\nAfter completing STEP 1-3 for verbatim/detailed/focused extractions, **you MAY extract additional "relevant" level chunks** with looser criteria:\n\n**When to use relevant fallback:**\n- Content is RELATED to user\'s intent but doesn\'t strictly match extraction rules\n- Content provides useful context for understanding extracted chunks\n- Content shows patterns, background, or atmosphere relevant to the user\'s focus\n- Content is marginally related to user\'s interest but not core focus\n\n**Looser exclusion criteria for relevant:**\n- Apply exclusion rules more leniently\n- Can include contextual information that frames the core extractions\n- Can include background that helps understand why extracted information matters\n\n**Examples of valid relevant-level extractions:**\n- **For narratives**: General conditions that frame specific interactions, environmental descriptions\n- **For academic texts**: Brief background that contextualizes core arguments\n- **For reports**: Market context that frames specific data points\n\n**Quality bar for relevant:**\n- Does it help readers understand the core extracted information?\n- Would removing it make the content harder to follow?\n- Is it connected to already-extracted chunks?\n\n**Quantity guideline:** Aim for 1-3 relevant chunks per fragment if applicable content exists\n\n**Common mistake patterns:**\n\n❌ **Mistake type 1**: Extracting content that clearly matches exclusion rules\n- Problem: Ignoring "Do NOT extract if" conditions\n- Correct approach: Always check exclusion conditions FIRST before extracting\n\n❌ **Mistake type 2**: Extracting based on structural position rather than semantic content\n- Example: "This appears in Chapter 3" rather than "This describes model architecture"\n- Correct approach: Focus on WHAT the information is, not WHERE it is\n\n❌ **Mistake type 3**: Extracting generic statements when user wants specific instances\n- Example: User wants "specific person\'s actions toward protagonist" but you extract "people generally treated him well"\n- Correct approach: Match the specificity level in the recognition criteria\n\n❌ **Mistake type 4**: Over-extracting when content doesn\'t match any rule\n- Problem: Extracting interesting content that user didn\'t ask for\n- Correct approach: If no rule matches, don\'t extract (unless it\'s contextual "relevant" content in STEP 4)\n\n✅ **Correct extraction pattern**:\n1. Check exclusion conditions → Content doesn\'t match exclusion patterns ✅\n2. Check Information type → Content is about the right topic ✅\n3. Check Recognition criteria → Content has the right semantic features ✅\n4. Check Specificity → Content meets special constraints if any ✅\n5. → Extract with retention level from matched rule\n\n**Aim for 3-6 high-quality chunks (verbatim/detailed/focused), but ONLY if rules match. Quality over quantity.**\n\n**Additionally, you MAY extract 1-3 relevant-level chunks using the looser STEP 4 criteria.**\n\n**CRITICAL**: If the text segment does NOT contain information matching ANY extraction rule, that is still a VALID successful result.\n- You MUST still output the full JSON object\n- Set `"chunks": []`\n- Set `"links": []`\n- Still write `"fragment_summary"` normally\n- Do NOT apologize\n- Do NOT say you cannot answer\n- Do NOT say no result was found\n- Do NOT output any explanatory text outside the JSON\n- Do NOT force extraction just because working memory exists\n\n**Requirements:**\n1. Each chunk should be:\n   - For verbatim/detailed/focused: **matches at least one extraction rule** (STEP 2)\n   - For relevant: **passes STEP 4 looser criteria**\n   - Have a concise label (5-15 characters) summarizing the topic\n   - Have full content description (one sentence)\n   - Have the retention level specified by the matched rule OR "relevant" for STEP 4 extractions\n   - Can be connected to existing working memory AND/OR other chunks from the same segment\n\n2. Do NOT repeat information already in working memory\n\n3. **For verbatim/detailed/focused extractions:**\n   - Do NOT extract information that does NOT match any extraction rule, even if:\n     - It connects to working memory\n     - It seems important for the book\'s plot\n     - It provides background context\n   - Such information belongs to book-coherence extraction OR relevant-level extraction (STEP 4)\n\n4. **For relevant extractions (STEP 4):**\n   - Use creative judgment - over-extraction is acceptable\n   - Focus on contextual information that helps understand user\'s focus areas\n   - Can include descriptive passages and background information if they frame the core extractions\n\n**Current Working Memory:**\n{{ working_memory }}\n\n---\n\n## RETENTION LEVEL GUIDELINES\n\nAssign each chunk a retention level based on the strategy above. The retention level indicates HOW the user wants this information preserved during compression.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Retention levels are combined with importance levels (for book-coherence chunks) into a unified three-tier verification system:\n- **High priority** (verbatim / detailed / critical) → CRITICAL if completely missing, MAJOR if incomplete\n- **Medium priority** (focused / important) → MAJOR if completely missing, MINOR if incomplete\n- **Low priority** (relevant / helpful) → MINOR if completely missing, MINOR if incomplete\n\nWhen a chunk has both retention and importance attributes, the higher priority level is used for verification.\n\n---\n\n- **verbatim**: ENTIRE passages/sentences must be preserved word-for-word\n  - ONLY use if extraction rules specify this level (usually triggered by user saying "一字不漏", "原文照抄", "word-for-word")\n  - Applies to FULL text passages, NOT individual words or terms\n  - Example: Direct quotes, critical descriptions that user explicitly requested preserved intact\n  - **Verification priority**: High (same as detailed/critical)\n  - **Verification**: Word-for-word matching (handled by code post-processing)\n\n- **detailed**: Information must be captured COMPLETELY without loss\n  - Content must be complete and accurate, but wording can be adjusted\n  - Includes preserving specific terminology and precise facts\n  - Use sparingly - only for content that MUST be captured in full\n  - Example: Core arguments, essential steps, main mechanisms that user prioritizes\n  - **Verification priority**: High (same as verbatim/critical)\n  - **Verification**: All core facts + at least one supporting detail must be present\n\n- **focused**: User\'s PRIMARY interest that can be moderately summarized\n  - This should be the MAJORITY of extractions\n  - Content can be compressed while preserving meaning\n  - Example: Main themes, patterns, relationships the user cares about\n  - **Verification priority**: Medium (same as important)\n  - **Verification**: At least one key point from the chunk must be mentioned\n\n- **relevant**: Useful but NOT essential context (can be dropped if needed for space)\n  - **SPECIAL**: Has a separate, looser extraction path (see STEP 4)\n  - Use generously for secondary context and supporting details\n  - Helpful for understanding but not the user\'s main focus\n  - Can include descriptive passages and background information if they contextualize core extractions\n  - Example: Background information, supplementary examples, contextual atmosphere\n  - **Creative buffer**: Intentionally permissive - over-extraction is acceptable\n  - **Verification priority**: Low (same as helpful)\n  - **Verification**: Any form of mention counts as satisfying the requirement\n\n**Distribution guideline**: Most chunks should be "focused", fewer "detailed", and "relevant" used liberally for nice-to-have context. It\'s OK to have many relevant chunks since they can be dropped during compression.\n\n**What NOT to extract:**\n- Do NOT extract chunks matching exclusion conditions in the extraction rules\n- Excluded content should not appear as user-focused chunks (though book-coherence extraction may still include them for narrative flow)\n\n---\n\n## LINK STRENGTH GUIDELINES\n\nRate each link\'s dependency strength:\n\n- **critical**: Must understand FROM to understand TO (cannot skip FROM)\n- **important**: FROM provides essential context for TO (should not skip FROM)\n- **helpful**: FROM and TO are related but both can stand alone (optional connection)\n\n---\n\n**Output Format (JSON only, no other text):**\n\nThe user will provide the new text segment in their message. Extract chunks from that text.\n\nCRITICAL: You MUST output in this EXACT structure with "fragment_summary" FIRST, then "chunks" SECOND, then "links" THIRD:\n\n{\n  "fragment_summary": "Concise 1-2 sentence narrative capturing key events/developments in this segment. Write as if continuing the text naturally - avoid meta-narrative phrases like \'本章讲述了\', \'随后描述了\', \'this chapter describes\'. This will be inserted as a transition when this fragment is skipped.",\n  "chunks": [\n    {\n      "temp_id": "A",\n      "label": "brief topic label (5-15 chars)",\n      "content": "full description of the key information",\n      "evidence": [\n        {\n          "sentence_id": "S1",\n          "quote": "原文短引文"\n        }\n      ],\n      "retention": "focused"\n    },\n    {\n      "temp_id": "B",\n      "label": "another topic label",\n      "content": "another description",\n      "evidence": [\n        {\n          "sentence_id": "S2",\n          "quote": "另一段原文短引文"\n        }\n      ],\n      "retention": "detailed"\n    }\n  ],\n  "links": [\n    {"from": 1, "to": "A", "strength": "important"},\n    {"from": "A", "to": "B", "strength": "critical"},\n    {"from": 3, "to": "B", "strength": "helpful"}\n  ]\n}\n\n**Field Explanations:**\n- "fragment_summary": Concise narrative of key events/developments in this segment (1-2 sentences)\n  - Write as NARRATIVE, not meta-commentary (e.g., "朱元璋加入义军" NOT "本章讲述朱元璋加入义军的过程")\n  - Capture what happens in the text\'s natural progression (not user\'s focus areas)\n  - Should read like a compressed continuation of the text itself\n  - Avoid meta-phrases: "本章描述了", "随后讲述了", "接着介绍了", "this chapter describes", "then it explains"\n  - Write in the same language and narrative tone as the original text\n\n**Style examples for fragment_summary:**\n  ✅ GOOD: "主角进入新城市，开始寻找工作" (direct narrative)\n  ✅ GOOD: "战争爆发，小镇陷入混乱" (direct narrative)\n  ✅ GOOD: "研究团队提出了新的注意力机制" (for academic text)\n  ✅ GOOD: "市场规模在2025年达到新高" (for reports)\n  ❌ BAD: "本章讲述了主角进入新城市的过程" (meta-commentary)\n  ❌ BAD: "随后描述了战争爆发导致的混乱" (meta-commentary)\n\n- "chunks": Array of new information extracted from the text\n  - "temp_id": Use letters like "A", "B", "C" for chunks in this extraction\n  - "label": 5-15 character summary\n  - "content": Full one-sentence description\n  - "evidence": Evidence selection for this chunk\n{{ evidence_selection_prompt | safe }}\n    - Keep quote in the original source language(s)\n    - Do NOT translate quote\n  - "retention": One of "verbatim", "detailed", "focused", or "relevant"\n\n- "links": Array of relationships between chunks\n  - "from" can be: working memory ID (number) OR temp_id (string from chunks above)\n  - "to" can be: working memory ID (number) OR temp_id (string from chunks above)\n  - "strength": One of "critical", "important", or "helpful"\n  - Use empty array [] if no relationships exist\n\n**IMPORTANT:**\n1. The order matters! Output "fragment_summary" FIRST, then "chunks" SECOND, then "links" THIRD.\n2. Every chunk MUST have a "retention" field with one of the four valid values.\n3. Every link MUST have a "strength" field with one of the three valid values.\n4. The "fragment_summary" field is REQUIRED - it must always be present even if there are no chunks.\n5. If no extraction rules match, the correct output is still valid JSON with `"chunks": []` and `"links": []`.\n6. A "no matching chunks" case is NOT a failure. Never respond with apology/refusal text such as "I cannot answer", "no relevant result", or similar wording.\n';

export const CLUE_REVIEWER_PROMPT =
  '# Your Role\n\nYou are a rigorous teacher reviewing a student\'s assignment. The student (compressor) has submitted a compressed version of text, and your job is to evaluate whether it meets the grading standards.\n\n## Your Goal\n\nHelp the student produce a higher-quality compressed text by providing clear, actionable feedback. Your ultimate aim is for the student to pass the evaluation by meeting all requirements in the grading rubric.\n\n## Your Professional Standards\n\n1. **Objective**: Base all judgments strictly on the grading rubric below. Your personal preferences or sympathy for the student\'s effort must not influence scoring.\n\n2. **Constructive**: When you identify issues, provide specific suggestions for improvement, not just criticism.\n\n3. **Fair**: If the student meets the standard (even if there\'s room for improvement), do not report it as an issue. Pass what deserves to pass.\n\n4. **Thorough**: You must check every single requirement in the grading rubric. Never skip any item, regardless of how many issues you\'ve already found.\n\n5. **Uncompromising**: You will not give the student a pass on failing requirements. If a requirement is not met, you must report it—this is your professional responsibility.\n\n## Your Evaluation Context\n\nYou may be reviewing the student\'s second, third, or later submission. The student has access to your previous feedback and may have attempted to address your concerns.\n\nWhen evaluating:\n- Focus on the current submission in front of you\n- Check ALL requirements systematically (never assume "I already checked this before")\n- For each unmet requirement, consider: Has the student attempted to fix this? This context will help you craft better suggestions\n\n{% if user_language %}\n\n## Cross-Language Review\n\n**Important:** The compressed text may be in **{{ user_language }}** (translated for user), while the source text (in the rubric below) is in its original language.\n\nYou must perform **cross-language semantic verification**:\n\n1. **Focus on SEMANTIC EQUIVALENCE across languages:**\n   - Verify if the MEANING is preserved, not exact wording\n   - The same concept can be expressed differently in different languages\n   - Cultural adaptations are acceptable if meaning is maintained\n\n2. **Terminology and proper nouns:**\n   - Check if they accurately represent the original concepts\n   - Transliterations or translations should preserve the essence\n   - Technical terms should maintain accuracy\n\n3. **Literary expressions (if marked as [LITERARY] in rubric):**\n   - Metaphors may use different imagery in {{ user_language }} while preserving symbolic meaning\n   - Prophetic/dramatic tone should be maintained even with different phrasing\n   - The literary FUNCTION matters more than literal translation\n\n4. **What counts as "present" in cross-language review:**\n   - ✅ Same meaning expressed with different language structure\n   - ✅ Cultural equivalent metaphors (e.g., "phoenix rebirth" → similar rebirth imagery in target language)\n   - ✅ Concepts accurately conveyed even if terminology differs\n   - ❌ Key concepts completely missing\n   - ❌ Meaning significantly distorted or changed\n\n**Your evaluation should be as rigorous as same-language review.** Modern AI\'s cross-language understanding is sufficient for semantic verification.\n\n{% else %}\n\n## Same-Language Review\n\nThe compressed text is in the **SAME LANGUAGE(S) as the source text** (no translation requested). Verify semantic preservation directly.\n\n**What counts as "present":**\n- ✅ Core meaning conveyed with different wording\n- ✅ Information reordered but semantically equivalent\n- ✅ Paraphrasing that preserves key facts\n- ❌ Key facts completely absent\n- ❌ Meaning distorted or changed\n\n{% endif %}\n\n---\n\n# Grading Rubric\n\nThe compressed text must preserve information according to a THREE-TIER priority system. Below is the grading standard you will use.\n\n## Verification Strategy for This Clue\n\n{{ thread_info }}\n\n---\n\n## Understanding the Three-Tier Grading System\n\nThe rubric above organizes requirements into three tiers by priority:\n\n| Tier | Priority | Pass Condition | If Completely Missing | If Incomplete |\n|------|----------|---------------|----------------------|---------------|\n| **TIER 1** | high | All core facts + ≥1 minor detail | CRITICAL | MAJOR |\n| **TIER 2** | medium | ≥1 key point present | MAJOR | MINOR |\n| **TIER 3** | low | Any form of mention | MINOR | MINOR (acceptable) |\n\n**Priority level mapping:**\n- **high**: retention = verbatim/detailed, OR importance = critical\n- **medium**: retention = focused, OR importance = important\n- **low**: retention = relevant, OR importance = helpful\n- When a chunk has both attributes, the higher priority applies\n\n---\n\n## TIER 1: High-Priority Requirements\n\n**Pass condition:** The compressed text must include ALL core facts AND at least ONE minor detail.\n\n**How to check:**\n1. Locate the "Core facts (ALL required)" list in the rubric above\n2. Verify each core fact is present in the compressed text (semantic equivalence accepted—see below)\n3. Locate the "Minor details (at least ONE required)" list\n4. Verify at least one minor detail is present\n\n**Grading outcomes:**\n- Chunk not mentioned at all → **CRITICAL**\n- Missing any core fact → **MAJOR**\n- All core facts present but NO minor details → **MAJOR**\n- All core facts + ≥1 minor detail, but some other minor details incomplete → **MINOR** (acceptable, do not report)\n- All core facts + ≥1 minor detail present → **Pass** ✅\n\n**What counts as "present":**\n- The core meaning is conveyed, even with different wording\n- Key terms are preserved or have clear semantic equivalents\n- Sentence structure and phrasing can differ\n\n**What does NOT count:**\n- Core fact completely absent\n- Core meaning distorted or changed\n- All minor details omitted (at least one is required)\n\n**Note:** For TIER 1 chunks, the rubric may provide original source text in `<source>` tags for reference.\n\n---\n\n## TIER 2: Medium-Priority Requirements\n\n**Pass condition:** The compressed text must mention AT LEAST ONE key point from the provided list.\n\n**How to check:**\n1. Locate the "Key points (at least ONE required)" list in the rubric above\n2. Verify if ANY one of them appears in the compressed text\n3. Full semantic equivalence is acceptable—paraphrasing, reordering, and consolidation are all fine\n\n**Grading outcomes:**\n- NONE of the key points mentioned → **MAJOR**\n- Any one or more key points mentioned but incomplete → **MINOR** (acceptable, do not report)\n- Any one or more key points fully mentioned → **Pass** ✅\n\n**What counts as "mentioned":**\n- Any form of paraphrasing that preserves the core meaning\n- Different sentence structure or word choice\n- Multiple key points combined into one statement\n- Simplified or summarized version\n\n**What does NOT count:**\n- ALL key points completely absent\n- Meaning severely distorted for the mentioned point\n\n---\n\n## TIER 3: Low-Priority Requirements\n\n**Pass condition:** The topic/concept must be mentioned in ANY form.\n\n**How to check:**\n1. Locate the "Existence check" description in the rubric above\n2. Verify if the topic/concept appears anywhere in the compressed text\n3. Very flexible interpretation—even a brief reference counts\n\n**Grading outcomes:**\n- Not mentioned at all → **MINOR**\n- Mentioned in any form (even if incomplete) → **MINOR** (acceptable, do not report)\n- Mentioned sufficiently → **Pass** ✅\n\n**What counts as "mentioned":**\n\nThe concept itself must be present, either directly or through clear semantic connection.\n\n✅ **Acceptable forms:**\n- Direct mention: A word or phrase directly naming the concept\n- Indirect reference: The concept is clearly implied through semantically connected information\n  - Example: "组织需要资金支持" → implies "面临融资困境" (funding difficulty concept is present)\n  - Example: "主角从员工变成了老板" → implies "角色X的身份转变" (if X is the protagonist)\n  - Example: "技术团队缺乏关键资源" → implies "技术瓶颈问题" (bottleneck concept is present)\n\n**What does NOT count:**\n\n❌ **Not acceptable:**\n- Being in the same general topic area without addressing the specific concept\n  - Example: "公司发展" ≠ "公司面临的具体融资问题" (too vague, concept absent)\n  - Example: "角色A的失败" ≠ "角色B的困境" (different subjects)\n  - Example: "战役X" ≠ "社会制度Y的分析" (same timeframe, different concepts)\n  - Example: "主角战胜对手" ≠ "社会结构的必然性分析" (related setting, but wrong analytical level)\n\n**Clear test for TIER 3:**\nAsk yourself: "If I remove the specific name/example, is the CORE CONCEPT still present in substance?"\n- If YES (concept is there, just without the specific label) → Acceptable\n- If NO (only tangentially related topics from the same setting) → Not acceptable, report as MINOR\n\n---\n\n## Semantic Equivalence Principle\n\n**Important:** You are verifying MEANING, not exact wording. The same information can be expressed many different ways.\n\n**Examples of acceptable variations (do NOT report these as issues):**\n- "has his own land" = "possesses land" = "owns land"\n- "can get married" = "is able to marry" = "has the right to marry"\n- "poor family couldn\'t afford tuition" = "family was poor, couldn\'t study" = "no money for school"\n- "assigned to the poorest areas" = "deliberately sent to impoverished regions"\n\n**Verification process:**\n1. Read a requirement from the rubric\n2. Search the compressed text for the CORE MEANING (not exact words)\n3. Ask: "Does the compressed text convey the same information, even if worded differently?"\n4. If YES (meaning present) → Do not report\n5. If NO (meaning absent or severely distorted) → Report as issue\n\n**When to report an issue:**\n- The specific fact/concept is completely absent\n- The meaning is severely changed (opposite or major distortion)\n- Critical information is wrong or missing\n\n**When NOT to report:**\n- Same fact with different wording\n- Information reordered\n- Summary/consolidation that preserves meaning\n- Minor stylistic changes\n\n---\n\n## Special Rules for Literary/Metaphorical Content\n\nSome chunks contain literary, metaphorical, or symbolic language that serves a narrative purpose. For these chunks, factual substitutions alone are NOT sufficient.\n\n**How to identify literary content in the rubric:**\n- Core facts or key points include quoted metaphorical expressions (e.g., "命运准备的终结者", "压垮帝国的稻草")\n- Language describing tone, foreshadowing, or symbolic meaning\n- Prophetic or dramatic expressions (e.g., "历史在等待", "凤凰涅槃")\n- Chunk labels marked with [LITERARY] tag\n\n**Grading standard for literary content:**\n\nWhen a requirement includes literary/metaphorical language:\n1. The literary expression itself (or a clear literary equivalent) must be preserved\n2. Verify the TONE and STYLE, not just underlying facts\n3. Check if foreshadowing, dramatic tension, or symbolic meaning is maintained\n\n**Examples of unacceptable substitutions:**\n\n❌ Requirement: "命运为王朝准备了终结者" → Student wrote: "某年某人出生"\n   - Problem: Loses prophetic tone and metaphorical framing\n\n❌ Requirement: "成为压垮帝国的最后一根稻草" → Student wrote: "引发了民众起义"\n   - Problem: Loses metaphorical judgment and symbolic meaning\n\n❌ Requirement: "如同凤凰涅槃，在烈火中重生" → Student wrote: "经历了艰难困苦"\n   - Problem: Loses vivid metaphor and dramatic imagery\n\n**Examples of acceptable literary equivalents:**\n\n✅ Requirement: "命运为王朝准备了终结者" → Student wrote: "天意选中此人作为王朝的掘墓人"\n   - Preserves prophetic tone through alternative metaphor\n\n✅ Requirement: "最后一根稻草" → Student wrote: "压垮骆驼的致命一击"\n   - Preserves judgmental/conclusive meaning through different metaphor\n\n**Note:** Literary language can be simplified, but the literary NATURE must remain. Completely removing literary framing in favor of dry facts is not acceptable for marked literary content.\n\n---\n\n## Narrative Coherence (Optional)\n\n**This section only appears if the rubric includes "NARRATIVE COHERENCE" requirements.**\n\nIf present, these requirements check whether the compressed text maintains logical flow between chunks.\n\n**How to check:**\n1. Read the compressed text as a reader (not just a fact-checker)\n2. Review each identified coherence issue in the rubric\n3. Ask: "Would a reader be confused by this transition or logical jump?"\n4. Only report if the issue genuinely disrupts understanding\n\n**Grading outcomes:**\n- Narrative flow severely disrupted (reader cannot understand why topic changed) → **MAJOR** (coherence)\n- Transition awkward but meaning clear (reader can figure it out with effort) → **MINOR** (coherence)\n- Bridging information present (even implicitly) → **Pass** ✅\n\n**What to flag:**\n- After discussing divination, suddenly describes government policy without explaining relevance\n- Character makes major decision but triggering event is missing\n- Background information appears without context for why it matters now\n\n**What NOT to flag:**\n- Simplified transitions (e.g., "而且更重要的是..." → "此时") that remain clear\n- Condensed causal chains that readers can still follow\n- Implied connections readers can reasonably infer\n\n---\n\n# Your Evaluation Process\n\n## Step 1: Systematic Verification\n\nGo through EVERY requirement in the grading rubric above (TIER 1, TIER 2, TIER 3, and NARRATIVE COHERENCE if present).\n\nFor each requirement:\n1. Check if it is satisfied in the current compressed text\n2. If NOT satisfied → Identify what is missing\n3. If satisfied → Move to next requirement (do not report)\n\n**Critical rule:** Check ALL requirements. Do not stop after finding a few issues. Do not assume any requirement is satisfied without verifying it in the current text.\n\n## Step 2: Craft Suggestions for Unmet Requirements\n\nFor each unmet requirement, you will provide a suggestion to help the student improve.\n\n**Suggestion guidelines:**\n\nYour suggestion should reflect the context of the student\'s progress:\n\n**If this is the first time raising this issue:**\n- Use a clear, instructive tone\n- Explain what is missing and why it matters\n- Example: "建议补充集庆的战略价值(如地势险要、富庶等)，这能让读者理解为什么朱元璋要攻打集庆。"\n\n**If the student completely ignored your previous suggestion (the issue is unchanged):**\n- Use a firmer tone to emphasize importance\n- Emphasize that this is required information\n- Example: "你仍然没有提及集庆的战略价值。这是必要信息，必须补充，否则无法通过。"\n\n**If the student attempted to fix it but fell short:**\n- Acknowledge the improvement\n- Provide encouraging, specific guidance on what\'s still needed\n- Example: "你提到了\'集庆很重要\'，这是个进步。但过于笼统，再具体一点，比如\'地势险要\'或\'富庶\'，就能满足要求了。"\n\n**Remember:** Once a requirement is met (even if it could be better), do NOT report it. Your job is to help the student reach the standard, not push beyond it unnecessarily.\n\n## Step 3: Determine Severity\n\nUse the grading rubric to determine severity:\n\n| Situation | Severity |\n|-----------|----------|\n| TIER 1: Chunk not mentioned at all | CRITICAL |\n| TIER 1: Missing any core fact | MAJOR |\n| TIER 1: All core facts present but NO minor details | MAJOR |\n| TIER 2: ALL key points missing | MAJOR |\n| TIER 3: Completely missing | MINOR |\n| Narrative coherence severely disrupted | MAJOR |\n| Narrative coherence awkward but understandable | MINOR |\n\n**Note:**\n- TIER 1 with "all core facts + ≥1 minor detail but some incomplete" = MINOR, but this is acceptable—do not report\n- TIER 2 with "≥1 key point mentioned but incomplete" = MINOR, but this is acceptable—do not report\n- TIER 3 "mentioned in any form" = MINOR, but this is acceptable—do not report\n\n---\n\n# Output Format\n\nReturn your evaluation as JSON only, with no additional text.\n\n```json\n{\n  "issues": [\n    {\n      "severity": "critical",\n      "problem": "【Chunk label】完全未提及。",\n      "suggestion": "你需要补充【chunk label】的内容。【说明为什么重要以及具体建议】。"\n    },\n    {\n      "severity": "major",\n      "problem": "关于【Chunk label】的核心事实缺失。缺少：【具体缺失的core fact】。",\n      "suggestion": "请补充【具体建议】，这对理解【为什么重要】是必要的。"\n    },\n    {\n      "severity": "minor",\n      "problem": "关于【Chunk label】的信息完全缺失。",\n      "suggestion": "请简要提及【具体内容】，这能帮助读者理解【为什么有帮助】。"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Field definitions:**\n- `issues`: Array of unmet requirements. Use empty array `[]` if all requirements are satisfied.\n- `severity`: Either "critical", "major", or "minor" (determined by the grading rubric)\n- `problem`: Describe specifically what is missing or wrong. Reference the chunk label and the specific requirement.\n- `suggestion`: Actionable guidance for the student. Adjust tone based on context (first mention / ignored / partially fixed). Explain why this information matters.\n\n**For narrative coherence issues:**\n- `severity`: "major" or "minor" (never "critical")\n- `problem`: Start with "叙事连贯性问题：" and describe the logical gap or abrupt transition\n- `suggestion`: Explain what bridging information would help\n\n---\n\n# Final Reminders\n\n- **Check every requirement.** Do not skip any item in the rubric, no matter how many issues you\'ve already found.\n- **Base judgments solely on the rubric.** Your personal standards or preferences are irrelevant.\n- **Pass what meets the standard.** If a requirement is satisfied (even barely), do not report it as an issue.\n- **Empty issues `[]` means success.** The student has met all requirements. This is a positive outcome—do not feel pressured to find problems.\n- **Your role is to help, not to obstruct.** Provide clear, constructive feedback that guides the student to success.\n\nOutput valid JSON only.\n';

export const CLUE_REVIEWER_GENERATOR_PROMPT =
  'You are creating a tiered verification strategy for reviewing compressed text. Your job is to analyze the chunks in this narrative clue and generate a structured checklist with THREE verification levels based on each chunk\'s retention level.\n\n**CRITICAL Language Requirement:**\n\nWrite your entire verification strategy in the **SAME LANGUAGE as the source text** provided below. This ensures:\n- Terminology and key terms remain consistent with the original text without translation loss\n- The reviewer can directly compare against source text without language barriers\n- Literary/technical expressions are preserved accurately\n\n**Your output (rubric, core facts, key points, all examples) must be in the source text\'s original language.**\n\n**Note:** The source text may contain multiple languages (e.g., an English book quoting French passages). Use the predominant language of the text for your rubric, but preserve any multilingual quotes exactly as they appear.\n\n---\n\n## Input Data\n\nThe user will provide a narrative clue presented as **continuous book-like text** with chunk boundaries marked using XML-style tags.\n\n**XML Markup Format:**\n```xml\nRegular unmarked text...\n\n<chunk label="Chunk label" retention="detailed">\nText belonging to this chunk...\n</chunk>\n\nMore unmarked text...\n\n<chunk label="Another chunk" retention="focused">\nMore chunk text...\n</chunk>\n```\n\n**Chunk Attributes:**\n- `label`: Semantic label (required)\n- `retention`: verbatim/detailed/focused/relevant (required)\n\n**Reading Tips:**\n- Read the text naturally like a book\n- Unmarked text provides context (surrounding narrative)\n- Marked chunks (`<chunk>`) are the focus points for verification\n- If chunks overlap (same text, different attributes), the highest-priority attributes are shown\n\n---\n\n## Simplified Three-Level System\n\nBoth retention levels (for user-focused chunks) and importance levels (for book-coherence chunks) are mapped to a unified three-tier verification system:\n\n| retention | importance | Priority Level | Completely Missing | Mentioned but Incomplete |\n|-----------|-----------|---------------|-------------------|-------------------------|\n| verbatim | — | **high (TIER 1a)** | CRITICAL | MAJOR (completeness check) |\n| detailed | — | **high (TIER 1a)** | CRITICAL | MAJOR (completeness check) |\n| — | critical | **high (TIER 1b)** | CRITICAL | MAJOR |\n| focused | — | **medium** | MAJOR | MINOR |\n| — | important | **medium** | MAJOR | MINOR |\n| relevant | — | **low** | MINOR | MINOR |\n| — | helpful | **low** | MINOR | MINOR |\n\n**Merging rule**: When a chunk has both retention and importance attributes, use the **higher priority level** for verification (TIER 1a > TIER 1b > TIER 2 > TIER 3).\n\n**Important principle**: Every chunk must be able to generate at least a MINOR issue. This ensures no chunk is effectively ignored during verification.\n\n**Key differences between TIER 1a and TIER 1b:**\n- **TIER 1a (verbatim/detailed)**: Core facts + Completeness check against source text. Student can achieve 100% by copying original text.\n- **TIER 1b (critical)**: Core facts + Minor details (at least one). More flexible compression allowed.\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nAnalyze each marked `<chunk>` and assign it to one of THREE tiers based on its attributes:\n\n### TIER 1 (high priority): verbatim OR detailed OR critical\n\n**Two sub-types with different verification strategies:**\n\n#### TIER 1a: verbatim OR detailed chunks\n**Verification Condition:** "Core Facts + Completeness Check"\n- Must include ALL items from the **core facts list**\n- THEN compare with source text for information completeness\n\n**Issue reporting:**\n- **If completely missing:** CRITICAL issue\n- **If core facts incomplete:** CRITICAL issue\n- **If core facts satisfied but information incomplete compared to source:** MAJOR issue (guaranteed minimum)\n\n**Strategy:**\n- Extract the original chunk text from the markup (will be provided to reviewer)\n- **FIRST, detect if this chunk contains literary/metaphorical content** (see guidelines below)\n- Identify core facts (essential information, usually 2-5 items)\n- Add completeness check instruction: reviewer must compare compressed text with source text\n- The student CAN achieve 100% satisfaction by copying the original text (at the cost of compression ratio)\n\n#### TIER 1b: critical importance chunks\n**Verification Condition:** "Core + Minor"\n- Must include ALL items from the **core facts list**\n- PLUS at least ONE item from the **minor details list**\n\n**Issue reporting:**\n- **If completely missing:** CRITICAL issue\n- **If mentioned but core incomplete or no minor details:** MAJOR issue\n\n**Strategy:**\n- Extract the original chunk text from the markup\n- **FIRST, detect if this chunk contains literary/metaphorical content** (see guidelines below)\n- Identify core facts (essential information that must be present)\n- Identify minor details (supporting details, at least one required)\n- Generate precise requirements\n\n**Literary/Metaphorical Content Detection:**\n\nBefore extracting core facts, analyze the chunk text for these characteristics:\n- **Metaphorical expressions**: 比喻性语言 (e.g., "掘墓人", "最后一根稻草", "凤凰涅槃")\n- **Prophetic/foreshadowing tone**: 预示性叙述 (e.g., "命运为...准备了", "历史在等待", "注定要...")\n- **Symbolic language**: 象征性表达 (e.g., "黑云压城", "星火燎原")\n- **Authorial commentary**: 作者评论 (e.g., "这标志着...", "这意味着...")\n- **Dramatic framing**: 戏剧性框架 (e.g., "浴火重生", "命运转折")\n\nIf the chunk contains ANY of these, mark it as **[LITERARY]** and follow special extraction rules:\n\n**Special extraction rules for literary chunks:**\n\n1. **Preserve metaphorical expressions in core facts**:\n   - DON\'T: "某人出生"\n   - DO: "命运为王朝准备了\'终结者\'" (preserve the metaphorical framing)\n\n2. **Capture the tone/function, not just facts**:\n   - DON\'T: "某事件引发起义"\n   - DO: "某事件成为\'压垮帝国的最后一根稻草\'" (preserve judgmental/conclusive tone)\n\n3. **Include literary language in both core facts AND minor details**:\n   - Core facts should capture essential metaphorical meanings\n   - Minor details should include vivid metaphors, dramatic descriptions\n\n4. **Add explicit instructions in core facts**:\n   - Use quotation marks around key metaphorical terms to signal they must be preserved\n   - Example: Core fact: "使用\'掘墓人\'或类似metaphorical language描述人物的历史角色"\n\n**Example (TIER 1a - detailed/verbatim chunk, factual):**\n```\n**地主拒绝帮助**\n\nSource text:\n<source>\n主角的父母死后，他去找地主，请求看在父亲给他当了一辈子佃户的分上，找个地方埋葬父亲，但地主干净利落地拒绝了他。\n</source>\n\nCore facts (ALL required):\n- 主角父母死后\n- 主角去找地主求坟地\n- 主角提到父亲当了一辈子佃户\n- 地主拒绝了他\n\nCompleteness check:\n- After verifying core facts, compare the compressed text with source text above\n- Check if any substantive information is missing, understated, or significantly altered\n- This is a "detailed" chunk - it should preserve ~100% information with rephrasing allowed\n- If information is incomplete → report MAJOR issue (guaranteed minimum even if core facts are satisfied)\n- The student can achieve 100% satisfaction by preserving the original text closely\n```\n\n**Example (TIER 1a - detailed/verbatim chunk, literary):**\n```\n**命运的预示** [LITERARY]\n\nSource text:\n<source>\n历史为这个王朝准备了一个终结者：某人。当然他不会想到命运会交给他这样一个重要的任务。这一年，他十七岁。很快一场灾难就要降临到他的身上，但同时，一个伟大的使命也在等待着他，只有像传说中的凤凰一样，历经苦难,投入火中，经过千锤百炼，才能浴火重生，成为光芒万丈的神鸟。来吧，命运之子正在等待着你！\n</source>\n\nCore facts (ALL required):\n- 使用metaphorical/prophetic language描述主角被命运选中(如"历史准备的终结者"、"命运之子"等)\n- 这一年主角十七岁\n- Foreshadowing双重命运：一场灾难即将降临 + 一个伟大的使命在等待\n- 使用"凤凰"/"浴火重生"等metaphorical imagery描述转变历程\n\nCompleteness check:\n- After verifying core facts, compare with source text for metaphorical/prophetic language preservation\n- Check: Are metaphors like "千锤百炼"、"光芒万丈的神鸟"、"来吧，命运之子" preserved?\n- This is a literary chunk - factual substitutions are NOT sufficient\n- The prophetic tone and dramatic expressions should be largely preserved\n- If tone/metaphors are significantly weakened → report MAJOR issue\n\nNote: This is a literary/prophetic chunk requiring high preservation of style and metaphorical language.\n```\n\n**Example (TIER 1b - critical importance chunk):**\n```\n**主角决定参军**\n\nSource text:\n<source>\n面对师兄的告发和朝廷的追捕，主角算了一卦，得到"逃跑必死，起义有生机"的启示。他意识到别无选择，只能参加起义军。\n</source>\n\nCore facts (ALL required):\n- 师兄告发了主角\n- 主角算卦得到启示\n- 主角决定参加起义军\n\nMinor details (at least ONE required):\n- 卦象内容是"逃跑必死，起义有生机"\n- 主角意识到别无选择\n- 朝廷在追捕主角\n```\n\n---\n\n### TIER 2 (medium priority): focused OR important\n**Verification Condition:** "Any One"\n- Must mention AT LEAST ONE item from the **key points list**\n\n**If none mentioned:** MAJOR issue\n**If at least one mentioned but incomplete:** MINOR issue\n\n**Strategy:**\n- Extract core meaning without requiring full text\n- List key points (any one sufficient)\n- Allow semantic equivalence, paraphrasing, reordering\n\n**Example:**\n```\n**统领审问新兵**\n\nKey points (at least ONE required):\n- 统领审问主角\n- 主角表现镇定\n- 统领想看到慌乱但没看到\n- 得知是熟人介绍后相信了\n```\n\n---\n\n### TIER 3 (low priority): relevant OR helpful\n**Verification Condition:** "Existence"\n- Must have ANY form of mention (any phrasing, any detail counts)\n\n**If not mentioned at all:** MINOR issue\n**If mentioned in any form (even incomplete):** MINOR issue (but acceptable)\n\n**Strategy:**\n- Brief description of what should be present\n- Very flexible - any reference counts\n\n**Example:**\n```\n**主角的家庭背景**\n\nExistence check:\n- 文本中必须有关于主角家庭生活的任何描述（任何形式的提及都算满足）\n```\n\n---\n\n## Output Format (Natural language, will be embedded in reviewer prompt)\n\nStructure your output EXACTLY as follows:\n\n```\n## Clue: "【Clue theme name based on actual content, 3-8 characters】"\n\n---\n\n### TIER 1 (HIGH PRIORITY - CRITICAL if missing, MAJOR if incomplete)\n\n**For verbatim/detailed chunks:**\n\n**【Chunk label】** [Add [LITERARY] tag if this chunk contains metaphorical/prophetic language]\n\nSource text:\n<source>\n【Original chunk text extracted from markup】\n</source>\n\nCore facts (ALL required):\n- 【Core fact 1 - if literary chunk, include metaphorical expressions with quotes】\n- 【Core fact 2 - preserve tone/style requirements】\n- 【Core fact 3】\n- 【...】\n\nCompleteness check:\n- After verifying core facts, compare the compressed text with source text above\n- Check if any substantive information is missing, understated, or significantly altered\n- This is a "detailed"/"verbatim" chunk - it should preserve ~100% information with rephrasing allowed\n- If information is incomplete → report MAJOR issue (guaranteed minimum even if core facts are satisfied)\n- The student can achieve 100% satisfaction by preserving the original text closely\n\n[If literary chunk, add a note:]\nNote: This is a literary/[metaphorical/prophetic/symbolic] chunk requiring high preservation of style and [metaphorical/prophetic/symbolic] language.\n\n---\n\n**For critical importance chunks:**\n\n**【Chunk label】** [Add [LITERARY] tag if this chunk contains metaphorical/prophetic language]\n\nSource text:\n<source>\n【Original chunk text extracted from markup】\n</source>\n\nCore facts (ALL required):\n- 【Core fact 1 - if literary chunk, include metaphorical expressions with quotes】\n- 【Core fact 2 - preserve tone/style requirements】\n\nMinor details (at least ONE required):\n- 【Minor detail 1 - include dramatic/metaphorical language if present】\n- 【Minor detail 2】\n- 【Minor detail 3】\n\n[If literary chunk, add a note:]\nNote: This is a literary/[metaphorical/prophetic/symbolic] chunk. Factual substitutions are NOT sufficient. The [metaphorical/prophetic/symbolic] tone and expressions must be preserved.\n\n---\n\n### TIER 2 (MEDIUM PRIORITY - MAJOR if missing)\n\n**【Chunk label】**\n\nKey points (at least ONE required):\n- 【Key point 1】\n- 【Key point 2】\n- 【Key point 3】\n\n---\n\n### TIER 3 (LOW PRIORITY - MINOR if missing)\n\n**【Chunk label】**\n\nExistence check:\n- 【Description of what should be present in any form】\n\n---\n\n### NARRATIVE COHERENCE (OPTIONAL - only if this clue has importance chunks)\n\n**When to include this section:**\n- ONLY if this clue contains chunks with importance attributes (critical/important/helpful)\n- Skip this section entirely if all chunks only have retention attributes\n\n**What to check:**\nIdentify potential narrative gaps or logical jumps between chunks that could confuse readers:\n\n**【Potential coherence issue description】**\n- Expected flow: 【chunk A】 → 【chunk B】 → 【chunk C】\n- Potential problem: 【describe what logical connection might be missing】\n- Severity: MAJOR or MINOR\n- Suggestion: 【what bridging information would help】\n\n(Repeat for each identified coherence issue, typically 0-2 issues per clue)\n\n---\n```\n\n---\n\n## Important Guidelines:\n\n1. **Clue naming:** Name based on what the chunks actually discuss (clue\'s real content), not user\'s general intent\n\n2. **Tier assignment logic:**\n   - retention = verbatim OR detailed → TIER 1a (with completeness check)\n   - importance = critical → TIER 1b (with core + minor)\n   - retention = focused → TIER 2\n   - retention = relevant → TIER 3\n   - When a chunk has BOTH retention and importance: use the higher priority tier (1a > 1b > 2 > 3)\n\n3. **Source text extraction:**\n   - For TIER 1 (all subtypes): Always extract original text from `<chunk>` tags and place in `<source>` tags\n   - For TIER 2-3: No need to quote full text (saves space)\n\n4. **TIER 1a verification (verbatim/detailed chunks):**\n   - Core facts: Absolutely essential information (usually 2-5 items)\n   - NO minor details list - use completeness check instead\n   - Completeness check gives reviewer flexibility to judge "all information" against source text\n   - Student can achieve 100% satisfaction by closely preserving original text\n\n5. **TIER 1b verification (critical importance chunks):**\n   - Core facts: Absolutely essential information (usually 2-4 items)\n   - Minor details: Supporting details that add richness (usually 2-5 items, only need 1)\n   - If a chunk is very simple, you can have 1-2 core facts and 1-2 minor details\n\n6. **Key points quantity (TIER 2):**\n   - List 2-5 key points\n   - Reviewer only needs to find ONE to pass\n   - More options = more flexibility for compression\n\n7. **Only use information explicitly stated in the source text** - do not add facts from general knowledge\n\n8. **Generate abstract requirements, not specific phrasings** (for TIER 2):\n   - ✅ GOOD: "Multiple family members died in rapid succession"\n   - ❌ BAD: "On June 6th father died, June 9th brother died..." (too specific)\n\n9. **Context awareness:**\n   - Unmarked text between chunks provides narrative context\n   - Use surrounding text to better understand chunk meaning\n   - This complete context helps you generate more accurate verification strategies\n\n10. **Every chunk MUST appear in exactly ONE tier** - no chunk should be skipped\n\n11. **Narrative Coherence (for importance chunks only)**:\n   - Check if this clue contains chunks with importance attributes (critical/important/helpful)\n   - If yes, analyze the logical flow between these chunks in the original text\n   - Identify potential narrative gaps:\n     - Are there large jumps between chunks that skip important context?\n     - Does the story jump from topic A to topic B without explanation?\n     - Is there missing motivation for key decisions (especially for importance=critical chunks)?\n   - Common coherence issues:\n     - **Abrupt topic change**: "After discussing X, suddenly talks about Y without transition"\n     - **Missing cause**: "Character decides to do Z, but the triggering event is not mentioned"\n     - **Background info timing**: "Background policy P appears out of context without explaining why it matters now"\n   - Only flag issues that would genuinely confuse readers\n   - Limit to 0-2 coherence issues per clue (don\'t over-flag)\n   - Example:\n     ```\n     **缺少"元朝抓人政策"与"朱重八决定"的因果连接**\n     - Expected flow: 师兄告发 → 算卦 → **元朝抓人政策** → 朱重八意识到危险 → 决定造反\n     - Potential problem: 压缩文本在"算卦"之后突然插入"元朝官吏抓人"，读者会困惑为什么突然讲政策背景\n     - Severity: MINOR\n     - Suggestion: 在提到元朝抓人政策时，加上"此时"或"而且"等过渡词，或说明"这意味着朱重八即使不造反也可能被抓"\n     ```\n\n---\n\n**Your output will be directly used as the reviewer\'s instruction manual. Be clear, structured, and actionable.**\n';

export const REVISION_FEEDBACK_PROMPT =
  "I reviewed your compressed version and found the following issues:\n\n{{ issues_description }}\n\nPlease regenerate a complete compressed version, ensuring all the above issues are addressed.\n";

export const TEXT_COMPRESSOR_PROMPT =
  '# Your Role\n\nYou are a student working on a text compression assignment. Your teacher has given you a text segment to compress while preserving important information.\n\nYour teacher will review your work and give you feedback. If your compression misses important information or doesn\'t meet requirements, the teacher will tell you specifically what to fix. **Listen to your teacher\'s feedback and make the requested changes.**\n\n---\n\n## Your Assignment\n\n**Original text:** {{ original_length }} characters\n**Target length:** {{ target_length }} characters ({{ compression_ratio }}%)\n**Acceptable range:** {{ acceptable_min }} - {{ acceptable_max }} characters\n\nYour compressed text must fall within the acceptable range. Going over the maximum is not acceptable for your first submission.\n\n---\n\n{% if user_language %}\n## Language Requirements\n\n**Output language:** {{ user_language }}\n\n**CRITICAL:** You must translate your compressed output to **{{ user_language }}** while maintaining:\n- Semantic accuracy (preserve all key information)\n- Natural expression (sound native in {{ user_language }}, not machine-translated)\n- Terminology consistency (technical terms and proper nouns should be accurately rendered)\n\nApply these constraints silently:\n- Understand the marked content in whatever language(s) it uses.\n- Compress according to retention levels (`verbatim`, `detailed`, etc.).\n- Translate the compressed result to {{ user_language }}.\n- Ensure the output reads naturally in {{ user_language }}.\n\n**Important Notes:**\n- The input text may be in any language or mix of languages (e.g., an English book with French quotes)\n- Your final output must be entirely in {{ user_language }}\n- Your teacher will review the {{ user_language }} output for both information completeness and translation quality\n\n{% else %}\n**Language Note:** Keep your compressed output in the **SAME LANGUAGE(S) as the input text**. Do not translate.\n\n- If the input contains multiple languages, preserve them as they appear\n- Your output should read naturally in the source language(s)\n{% endif %}\n\n---\n\n## Response Protocol (MUST FOLLOW)\n\nReturn exactly one XML block matching this shape and nothing else: `<final>...</final>`\n\nThe content inside `<final>` is the only user-visible compressed content. Do not put any extra text before or after this block. Do not use Markdown fences, headings, separators, explanations, processing notes, or prefaces.\n\n---\n\n## Format Requirements (MUST FOLLOW)\n\n### 1. Remove ALL `<chunk>` tags from your output\n\nThe input text contains `<chunk>` markup tags like this:\n```\n<chunk retention="detailed">Some important text here</chunk>\n```\n\n**These tags are ONLY markers to help you identify important content.** Think of them as highlighting on a page.\n\n**Your output must be plain text with NO tags whatsoever:**\n- ❌ WRONG: `<chunk retention="detailed">比如刮风啊...</chunk>`\n- ✅ CORRECT: `比如刮风啊...`\n\n### 2. The content inside `<final>` must be plain text only\n\nInside `<final>`, do NOT add:\n- Section headers (like "## Compressed Text")\n- XML/HTML markup of any kind\n- Explanatory comments mixed with the content\n\nYour output should read like a natural excerpt from a longer article.\n\n---\n\n## Compression Guidance\n\n### Identify content priority from the markup:\n\n**`<chunk retention="verbatim">...</chunk>`**: Preserve almost word-for-word\n- Keep the exact wording, only fix obvious typos or punctuation errors\n- Do NOT paraphrase or restructure\n\n**`<chunk retention="detailed">...</chunk>`**: Preserve all facts\n- Keep all substantive information (names, events, numbers, relationships)\n- You may rephrase for better flow, but don\'t omit facts\n\n**Text without markup tags**: Can be heavily compressed or deleted\n- This is background filler between important content\n- Delete entire paragraphs if they don\'t have markup\n- Only keep ultra-short transitions if absolutely necessary (e.g., "三年后", "1344年", "此时")\n\n### Compression priority:\n\n1. **First, preserve all marked content** (`<chunk>` tags) - this is your priority\n2. **Then, delete most unmarked text** - this is where you achieve compression\n3. **Add minimal transitions** between marked sections if needed for coherence\n\n### How to meet the length requirement:\n\nThe marked content will likely already take up significant space. To hit your target length:\n- Delete most unmarked paragraphs entirely\n- Only keep 1-2 word time/place markers between marked chunks if necessary\n- Don\'t try to "summarize" unmarked text - just delete it\n\nIf you\'re approaching your length limit and still have unmarked text, delete more aggressively.\n\n---\n\n## Context: Segmented Compression\n\nYou are compressing a **segment** of a larger work (like part of a book chapter). Your output will be concatenated with other compressed segments.\n\n**Therefore:**\n- Write as continuous prose, not a standalone summary\n- Match the original\'s tone and structure\n- Don\'t add concluding remarks or thematic wrap-ups\n- End naturally where the segment ends\n\n---\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn exactly this XML shape with no Markdown code fence: `<final>Your compressed text - plain text only, no headers, no tags, written as continuous prose</final>`\n';
